<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mark the Evangelist</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Saint Mark, St Mark</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mark the Evangelist is the traditional author of the Gospel of Mark and, by Christian tradition, founder of the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. In 828 two Venetian merchants smuggled relics said to be his out of Alexandria to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, where the Basilica of San Marco was built to house them. His winged lion became the emblem of the Venetian state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist.jpg" alt="Mark the Evangelist" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scriptorium of the Lorsch Abbey (Hofschule Karls des Großen), Germany · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codexaureus_21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
